  "worked_solution": "Recall that the linear correlation coefficient $\\rho $ satisfies the inequality\n\n$$\n-1\\le \\rho \\le 1,\n$$\n\nwhere $\\rho =1$ indicates a perfect positive correlation, and $\\rho =-1$ indicates a perfect negative correlation.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our scatter plot.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/differential/14226558/assets/74aa691e42eea766595eede7d42d59e164509d6dbf3c8a609dd42e1196d11512.png)\n\nWe note the following:\n\n- The points in the scatter plot seem to follow a straight line with a *negative* slope. So, the correlation coefficient must be *negative*. Thus, we disregard the following values:\n\n$$\n\\rho ≈0.8,\\,\\rho ≈0.4,\\,\\rho ≈0\n$$\n\n- The points are scattered quite far from the corresponding trend line. So, the correlation coefficient must be closer to $0.$ Thus, we disregard the following value:\n\n$$\n\\rho ≈-0.9\n$$\n\nTherefore, the best approximation of the correlation coefficient must be $\\rho ≈-0.3.$",
